.TH VIGIL-UNLOCK 8 2026-11-19 vigil "System Administration"
.SH NAME
vigil-unlock \- unlock a session that vigil-lock holds
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vigil-unlock
.I session-id
|
.I user
.SH DESCRIPTION
vigil-lock obeys a logind Unlock only when root asked for it.
logind lets the owner of a session unlock it without polkit, and the
Unlock signal does not say who asked.
The rule closes that route: a process the user runs can no longer unlock
the screen by asking logind.
Plain
.B loginctl unlock-session
no longer unlocks vigil-lock, whoever runs it.
.PP
The rule covers logind only.
A process running as the same user can still reach the compositor.
On Hyprland with
.B allow_session_lock_restore
set, such a process could kill vigil-lock, take the session lock with a
client of its own, and release it.
.PP
.B vigil-unlock
is the recovery path, for example from another terminal or over SSH:
.PP
.RS
.B sudo vigil-unlock
.I session-id
.RE
.PP
It must run as root.
It first removes any token in
.I /run/vigil/unlock
that is no longer fresh.
Then it writes a token for each chosen session to
.IR /run/vigil/unlock/ <session-id>,
calls logind's
.B UnlockSession
for it, waits two seconds, removes the token, and prints whether logind
reports the session unlocked.
.PP
A session id names that session, and it is an error when that session
cannot lock.
Otherwise the argument is a user name and names every session of that
user that can lock.
Sessions that cannot lock, such as the
.B user@.service
manager session, are skipped.
.B loginctl list-sessions
lists the ids.
.PP
If
.B vigil-unlock
is cut short before it removes a token, for example by Ctrl-C or a
dropped SSH connection, the token lapses 10 seconds after it was
written, and the next run removes it.
.SH THE TOKEN
vigil-lock reads the token through descriptors and accepts it only if
all of these hold:
.IP \(bu 2
it is a regular file, not a symlink, with one link, owned by root, with
mode 0644 or stricter;
.IP \(bu 2
its directory and that directory's parent are owned by root, nobody
else can write either, and neither is a symlink;
.IP \(bu 2
it names this session and this boot's id;
.IP \(bu 2
its time, read from CLOCK_BOOTTIME, is at most 10 seconds old and not
in the future.
.PP
Otherwise vigil-lock logs
.B unlock ignored:
and the reason, and stays locked.
A missing or bad token never ends the lock.
.PP
The package's tmpfiles.d entry creates
.I /run/vigil/unlock
at boot, root-owned with mode 0755.
.B vigil-unlock
creates it the same way when it is missing.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when every chosen session that can lock reports unlocked, 1 otherwise,
and 2 on a usage error.
.PP
The report comes from logind's
.B LockedHint
property, which the session's owner sets, so a process the user runs can
make it say unlocked while the screen stays locked.
Treat "unlocked" as advisory and look at the screen to be sure.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/vigil/unlock/
Root's unlock tokens.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR loginctl (1),
.BR tmpfiles.d (5)
